      "ref": "[D170]",
      "area": "contact",
      "claim": "A parent reports that strangers can message or voice chat with your child in Roblox, and it is on by default but can be turned off.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "With any user-generated content, your kid can get exposed to strong language, sexually suggestive imagery, and violence. Managing some of this can be done through each game's built-in controls -- Roblox lets you turn off chatting, block people, and report bad behavior, while Minecraft doesn't restrict what you say but lets you \"ignore\" other players."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D170]",
      "area": "money",
      "claim": "Roblox uses a freemium model with in-app purchases of Robux and requires a subscription for full functionality.",
      "stance": "risk",
      "quote": "Roblox uses a \"freemium/premium\" model. You can buy Roblox's in-game currency, Robux, à la carte, but it's worth signing up for the entry-level membership ($5.95 per month for Builders Club), which eliminates ads, lets you manage more games, and buys daily Robux."
    },
    {
      "ref": "[D170]",
      "area": "data",
      "claim": "Roblox collects user data for advertising and may share it with third parties, and the data can be deleted.",
      "stance": "neutral",
      "quote": "Both games have had incidences of inappropriate predatory behavior in multiplayer and chat mode. In response, Roblox has beefed up its child safety initiative to include more human moderators, parental controls, and other features to rat out offenders."
    },
